# Auto Commit & Push After Every Change

After completing ANY code change (file edit, creation, or deletion), you MUST immediately:

1. Stage all changes: `git add -A`
2. Commit with a clear, concise message describing what was changed and why
3. Push to origin: `git push origin main`

## Commit Message Format

Use this format — one line, present tense, under 72 characters:

```
<type>: <short description of what changed>
```

Types: `fix`, `feat`, `refactor`, `style`, `chore`

Examples:
- `fix: reload dashboard on back-nav so completed status reflects immediately`
- `feat: add manual completion with reason to refund progress screen`
- `fix: remove duplicate MANUALLY CLOSED badge in timeline`

## Rules

- ALWAYS run `git add -A` before committing — never skip untracked files
- NEVER skip the push step — changes must land on `origin main` after every session
- If push fails due to upstream divergence, run `git pull --rebase origin main` then push again
- Commit immediately after the last file edit in a logical change set — do not batch across unrelated features
- Do not commit secrets, `.env` files, or build artifacts (`build/`, `.gradle/`)

## Example Shell Sequence (PowerShell)

```powershell
git add -A
git commit -m "fix: reload dashboard list after returning from detail screen"
git push origin main
```
